        details = "Keeps: resident participation in budgets, from Commons governance and Participatory economics, as in Participatory budgeting in Porto Alegre, where the evidence is 22 interviews with no quantitative impact study. Blocks: abolition of elected councils, from Corporatism and fascist economics, by a legal guarantee that councils stay elected; splitting local government into new small municipalities, from Distributism, by a results test and a staffing floor before any boundary change. Test: each year for five years, measure each council's share of public spending, the share of its budget that residents decide, pupil results and staff turnover, against councils whose boundaries did not change. Source: Terence Wood dissertation on Porto Alegre and Belo Horizonte; Italian-language anthology on the podestà (2023), read only as excerpts; IDB study by Alcaíno and colleagues (2022), read only through a search summary.",
